<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/advanced/decolonisation-postcolonial-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Decolonisation is the story of <strong>empires ending — and of what they left behind</strong>.
    Between 1945 and 1975, more than 80 new nations replaced colonial rule, redrawing the world
    map. The aftermath — borders, languages, institutions, debts and diasporas — is the world we
    live in, which is why this field reads forward from independence as carefully as backward
    from conquest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="independence, its modes, and the inheritances" widget="equationStep" config={decolCfg} />

  <h2>The postcolonial framework</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Non-alignment:</strong> newly independent states refused both blocs (Bandung Conference 1955, 29 nations; NAM at its peak ~120 members) — the Cold War's third force, loudly declaring it wouldn't be a chessboard. (The chessboard disagreed.)</li>
    <li><strong>Postcolonial studies:</strong> Said's Orientalism (1978) argued the West's knowledge of "the East" was built as an instrument of rule — the book that moved colonialism's legacy from history departments into literature, sociology and politics.</li>
    <li><strong>Success and struggle vary enormously:</strong> Singapore and South Korea leapt to wealth; resource-curse states stayed trapped. Historians resist single explanations — starting conditions, institutions, leadership and luck all diverged.</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural decolonisation:</strong> language (should independent India keep English?), curriculum, museums (the repatriation debates) and names — the arguments happening right now are this history continuing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Said, Orientalism (1978); Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961); Acemoglu & Robinson on colonial institutions; UN decolonisation records.</p>
</TopicPage>
